-- T-003 — prepared for T-103 (Audit Write Path). NOT applied to anything
-- in T-003 itself, because the table it targets does not exist yet.
--
-- Architecture §14 (Constraints): "AuditLogEntry rows are insert-only at
-- the database permission level (no UPDATE/DELETE grant to the
-- application's normal runtime role) as a second layer under the
-- domain-level append-only rule (P5/P2)."
--
-- 003_default_privileges.sql gives app_runtime standard CRUD (SELECT,
-- INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) as the *default* for every future table, because
-- that's correct for ordinary business tables. Postgres default
-- privileges cannot single out one specific future table name in advance
-- (there is no such table to name yet) — so the Audit table's stricter
-- rule cannot be expressed until that table exists.
--
-- USAGE: whichever task creates the real Audit table (T-103 in the
-- current Backlog) must run this immediately after that CREATE TABLE,
-- with <<AUDIT_TABLE_NAME>> replaced by the real table name (Architecture
-- §12/§14 discusses the entity as `AuditLogEntry`; the physical table name
-- — snake_case, pluralized, etc. — is exactly the "later database-design
-- phase" detail Architecture explicitly defers, so it is NOT invented
-- here):
--
--   REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE ON <<AUDIT_TABLE_NAME>> FROM app_runtime;
--
-- app_runtime keeps SELECT (Audit is readable, e.g. for the Staff App
-- Audit Log screen, permission-gated at the application layer) and INSERT
-- (AppendAuditEntry, T-103) from the 003_default_privileges.sql baseline;
-- only UPDATE/DELETE are removed, matching "insert-only."
--
-- T-003's own verify script (../verify/verify_t003.sql) proves this exact
-- REVOKE mechanism works correctly against a disposable probe table, so
-- that when T-103 runs the one-liner above for real, it is applying an
-- already-tested pattern rather than an unverified one.

REVOKE UPDATE, DELETE ON <<AUDIT_TABLE_NAME>> FROM app_runtime;
